# DuDuClaw OS — Steam/game-controller device access rules.
#
# Ticket: Y5-2, formalizing a manual QEMU-session workaround from Y4-2 into
# a real image mechanism. Y4-2 traced Steam's Flatpak wrapper crash to
# `steam_wrapper.py`'s `check_device_perms()` (one-hand-verified against
# flathub/com.valvesoftware.Steam's actual source this round):
#
#   uinput_path = Path("/dev/uinput")
#   has_perms = os.access(uinput_path, os.R_OK | os.W_OK)
#   if not has_perms: MSG_NO_INPUT_DEV_PERMS.show()   # -> zenity, see below
#
# This is a REAL os.access() check, not a "is the steam-devices package
# installed" heuristic — without a rule that changes /dev/uinput's group
# ownership, the device stays whatever the kernel's default is (root:root,
# mode 0600 on a stock `uinput` misc-device registration) regardless of
# which supplementary groups the calling user has, which is exactly the dead
# end Y4-2 hit ("即使把 verify 使用者加進 input 群組...也沒有解掉" — true,
# because nothing had yet told udev to put the DEVICE itself in that group).
#
# Upstream reference, one-hand-verified this round:
# https://github.com/ValveSoftware/steam-devices/blob/master/60-steam-input.rules
# (the actual content Debian's real `steam-devices` package ships — ~130
# rules for every controller vendor Valve has ever special-cased). That
# upstream file uses `TAG+="uaccess"` almost everywhere: udev's builtin ACL
# mechanism that grants access to whichever user systemd-logind currently
# has as the ACTIVE SEAT SESSION. THIS distro's kiosk model (duduclaw-
# kiosk.service, duduclaw-flatpak-kiosk-verify.service) never creates a
# logind session at all — both are plain systemd system services with a
# static User=, no PAM/logind login ever happens — so `uaccess` alone would
# silently grant nobody anything here, the same class of gap
# duduclaw-polkit-flatpak's own rule file already had to work around for
# polkit's `subject.active` check. Batocera (Buildroot, same "fixed system
# user, no seat session" shape as this OS) independently reaches the same
# conclusion in production: its `99-steam-controller.rules` uses
# `GROUP="input"` instead of `TAG+="uaccess"` for exactly this reason
# (research/native-os-2026-08/flatpak-carrier-2026-08.md §4.3, one-hand
# verified against Batocera's own master branch).
#
# Scope decision (deliberately NOT vendoring the full ~130-line upstream
# list): this milestone's judgment criterion is "Steam client reaches a
# login screen", which needs exactly ONE rule — /dev/uinput, the one
# check_device_perms() actually tests. The Valve USB/hidraw vendor rules
# below cover Valve's own controllers (Steam Controller, Steam Deck, Steam
# Link) since they are literally what the package is named for and cost
# nothing extra to carry. Third-party controller support (Xbox/PlayStation/
# Nintendo/Razer/etc., the bulk of upstream's list) is explicitly OUT of
# scope here and left as a follow-up for whenever real controller support
# is a judgment criterion — see the TODO doc's Y5-2 row. Every rule below
# additionally sets GROUP="input" (not just TAG+="uaccess") so it actually
# does something under this OS's no-logind kiosk model; the uaccess tag is
# kept alongside it at zero cost, in case a future logind/seat-based
# interactive-login mode is ever added to this OS.

# Steam Controller / any uinput-backed virtual gamepad Steam itself creates
# (SteamInput's "Steam Controller" is a uinput virtual device, not a real
# USB one) — this is the SPECIFIC device check_device_perms() tests.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", MODE="0660", GROUP="input", TAG+="uaccess", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"

# Valve USB devices (Steam Controller wireless receiver, Steam Deck, Steam
# Link, etc. — idVendor 28de is Valve's USB-IF vendor ID).
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="28de", MODE="0660", GROUP="input", TAG+="uaccess"

# Valve HID devices over USB hidraw.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="28de", MODE="0660", GROUP="input", TAG+="uaccess"

# Valve HID devices over Bluetooth evdev.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{id/vendor}=="28de", MODE="0660", GROUP="input", TAG+="uaccess"
